WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.716
Teacher midterm practice for C plus plus, question 24.

2
00:00:05.336 --> 00:00:08.418
The table needs exactly eleven rows, for x

3
00:00:08.418 --> 00:00:11.835
from zero to one in steps of zero point one.

4
00:00:11.835 --> 00:00:16.726
Why is a loop over int i from zero to ten, with x equal to zero point

5
00:00:16.726 --> 00:00:21.550
one times i, safer than adding zero point one to x each time?

6
00:00:23.251 --> 00:00:25.452
Zero point one has no exact binary

7
00:00:25.452 --> 00:00:29.853
representation, just like one third has no exact decimal.

8
00:00:29.853 --> 00:00:32.830
So every addition carries a tiny error.

9
00:00:33.449 --> 00:00:36.413
After ten additions, x is zero point nine nine

10
00:00:36.413 --> 00:00:41.096
nine, nine nine nine, and so on, eight nine. Not one.

11
00:00:41.096 --> 00:00:46.549
So a test like x less than or equal to one can give one row too many, or too few.

12
00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:51.065
With an int counter, the row count is exact, and x equals

13
00:00:51.065 --> 00:00:54.361
zero point one times i has no accumulated error.

14
00:00:55.961 --> 00:00:58.930
So the int counter is safer because zero point one

15
00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:02.136
is not exact in binary, and the sum drifts.

16
00:01:02.956 --> 00:01:05.752
Using equals equals, or not equals, with an

17
00:01:05.752 --> 00:01:08.494
accumulated double as a loop end condition.

18
00:01:09.215 --> 00:01:12.597
Exam tip: count with integers, and compute with doubles.
